@startuml proto-nec-ir-frame
' ===========================================================================
' proto-nec-ir-frame.puml - (b) the NEC 32-bit frame, its repeat code, and what
' this act actually puts on the wire.
'
' A REAL NEC remote frame is 32 bits, LSB first, in four bytes, each followed by
' its one's complement so a receiver can reject a frame corrupted in the light
' path:
'   address   ~address   command   ~command
'
' and the auto-repeat is a special 16-bit sequence: command 0x00 followed by
' 0xFF, inside the SAME leader, sent ~40 ms after the original while the key is
' held. That is a real NEC feature and the frame figure has to show it, because
' "what does a repeat code look like" is a question this diagram exists to answer.
'
' WHAT THIS ACT SENDS IS NOT 32 BITS. firmware/nec_ir.pe sends a valid NEC FRAME
' ENVELOPE - leader, leader gap, eight bit-slots, stop burst - around a single
' payload byte, 0xA5. The 32-bit payload with its inverse bytes is NOT exercised.
' The figure says which is which rather than drawing a 32-bit frame the firmware
' does not produce.
' ===========================================================================

title NEC infrared - the frame grammar, the repeat code, and what this act sends\n<font size=12>the protocol's frame is 32 bits; this act sends the envelope around ONE byte</font>

hide empty description
hide stereotype
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam rectangle {
  BorderColor #33555
  FontName monospaced
  FontSize 12
}
skinparam rectangle<<gate>> {
  BackgroundColor #FFF4D6
  BorderColor #C8B800
}
skinparam rectangle<<sent>> {
  BackgroundColor #D6F5D6
  BorderColor #3A7A3A
}
skinparam rectangle<<notsent>> {
  BackgroundColor #EFEFEF
  BorderColor #999999
}
skinparam rectangle<<real>> {
  BackgroundColor #F4F7FB
}

rectangle "LEADER\n\n<b>9 ms</b> of 38 kHz burst\n\n<b>measured 8 988.0 us</b>\nin <b>343</b> carrier cycles\n\nsent as IR_LEADR runs of\nIR_LEAD cycles, because the\nEIGHT-BIT IMMEDIATE cannot\nhold 342" as L <<sent>>
rectangle "LEADER GAP\n\n<b>4.5 ms</b> of silence\n\n<b>measured 4 504.0 us</b>\n\nthis gap is what tells a\nreceiver a frame is STARTING\nrather than continuing" as G <<sent>>

rectangle "SLOT 0\nbit 0 of 0xA5 = 1" as S0 <<sent>>
rectangle "SLOT 1\nbit 1 of 0xA5 = 0" as S1 <<sent>>
rectangle "SLOT 2\nbit 2 of 0xA5 = 1" as S2 <<sent>>
rectangle "SLOT 3\nbit 3 of 0xA5 = 0" as S3 <<sent>>
rectangle "SLOT 4\nbit 4 of 0xA5 = 0" as S4 <<sent>>
rectangle "SLOT 5\nbit 5 of 0xA5 = 1" as S5 <<sent>>
rectangle "SLOT 6\nbit 6 of 0xA5 = 0" as S6 <<sent>>
rectangle "SLOT 7\nbit 7 of 0xA5 = 1" as S7 <<sent>>

rectangle "STOP BURST\n\n<b>552.0 us</b> in 22 cycles\n\nthen the pad is released\n\n<b>WITHOUT IT A RECEIVER CANNOT</b>\n<b>TELL THE FRAME ENDED from a</b>\n<b>very long gap</b>" as ST <<sent>>

rectangle "RELEASE\n\ndmem[14] = 0:\nthe frame is out" as P <<gate>>

L -[#3A7]-> G
G -[#3A7]-> S0
S0 -[#3A7]-> S1
S1 -[#3A7]-> S2
S2 -[#3A7]-> S3
S3 -[#3A7]-> S4
S4 -[#3A7]-> S5
S5 -[#3A7]-> S6
S6 -[#3A7]-> S7
S7 -[#3A7]-> ST
ST -[#3A7]-> P

note bottom of S0
  <b>A 0 and a 1 have the SAME burst and different gaps.</b>
  ...
  | bit | burst | gap | slot |
  | <b>0</b> | 552.0 us | <b>1 694.9 us</b> (datasheet 1 687.5) | 2 246.9 us |
  | <b>1</b> | 552.0 us | <b>562.2 us</b> (datasheet 562.5) | 1 114.2 us |
  ...
  The burst is IDENTICAL, so the value is carried entirely by the silence. There
  is no level to read and no clock to resynchronise to, which is why this is the
  sharpest timing claim in the family: a gap 3 % long is still a zero, and a gap
  30 % short is not a one.
end note

note bottom of S7
  <b>Why 0xA5 and nothing else.</b>
  Both bit values appear in the byte, and there are six transitions in eight. Its
  bit-reverse is 0xA5 - it is a palindrome - and its complement is 0x5A, and
  NEITHER is anything a wrong bit order produces by accident. So a firmware that
  reversed the byte, complemented it, or rotated it cannot pass.
end note

rectangle "THE PROTOCOL'S REAL 32-BIT FRAME - NOT EXERCISED BY THIS ACT" as HDR <<real>>
rectangle "ADDRESS\n8 bits, LSB first" as AD <<notsent>>
rectangle "~ADDRESS\n8 bits, the one's complement" as NAD <<notsent>>
rectangle "COMMAND\n8 bits, LSB first" as CM <<notsent>>
rectangle "~COMMAND\n8 bits, the one's complement" as NCM <<notsent>>

HDR -[#99A,hidden]-> AD
AD -[#99A]-> NAD
NAD -[#99A]-> CM
CM -[#99A]-> NCM

note right of HDR
  A real NEC frame is <b>32 bits</b> in four bytes, each byte followed by its
  one's complement, so a receiver can reject a frame corrupted on the way through
  the air. The four are the ADDRESS of the remote and the COMMAND of the key, and
  the inverses are the only error detection NEC has - there is no checksum, no
  acknowledge and no retry.

  <b>firmware/nec_ir.pe sends ONE byte, not four.</b> The envelope
  (leader, gap, eight slots, stop burst) is a correct NEC frame, and the payload
  in it is 0xA5. So what this act proves is the FRAMING and the CARRIER; the
  32-bit payload with its inverse bytes is not exercised, and the figure marks
  those four boxes as unsent rather than drawing a frame the firmware does not
  produce.
end note

rectangle "THE REPEAT CODE - NOT EXERCISED BY THIS ACT" as RH <<real>>
rectangle "LEADER + GAP\n<b>again</b>, about 40 ms\nafter the original" as RL <<notsent>>
rectangle "COMMAND = 0x00\n8 zero bits" as RC0 <<notsent>>
rectangle "~COMMAND = 0xFF\n8 one bits" as RC1 <<notsent>>

RH -[#99A,hidden]-> RL
RL -[#99A]-> RC0
RC0 -[#99A]-> RC1

note right of RH
  NEC's auto-repeat is a <b>different 16-bit sequence, not a repeat of the
  32</b>. While a key is held the remote re-sends a leader, a gap, and then
  command <b>0x00</b> with its inverse <b>0xFF</b> - and the inverse is what
  makes it unambiguous, because 0x00 is also a legitimate command code. A
  receiver tells "held" from "pressed" by the 0x00/0xFF pair and by the ~40 ms
  spacing, not by the byte count.

  This act does not send it. The figure shows it because "how does a repeat code
  differ from a frame" is the question the NEC frame diagram has to answer, and
  answering it with only the frame would leave the question open.
end note

legend bottom
  |= part |= this act | the protocol |=
  | leader, leader gap | <b>SENT</b> (green) | same | 
  | 8 bit-slots, LSB first | <b>SENT</b> (green) | same, 32 slots in a real frame |
  | stop burst | <b>SENT</b> (green) | same |
  | 32-bit payload: address, ~address, command, ~command | <b>NOT SENT</b> (grey) | required |
  | the 0x00 / 0xFF repeat | <b>NOT SENT</b> (grey) | required for auto-repeat |

  <b>Frame length, and why a period error cannot make the frame
  internally inconsistent.</b> One byte is 32.06 ms: 9 + 4.5 + 8 x 2.25 + 0.5625.
  The burst and the gap are independent counts, so an error in the carrier shows
  up as a wrong frequency and an error in a gap shows up as a wrong slot - and
  neither can quietly cancel the other inside one frame.

  <b>Green means measured in this simulation. Grey means the protocol has it and
  this act does not send it.</b> The distinction is drawn here rather than left
  to the reader, because a diagram that draws the protocol's frame and does not
  say which parts the firmware produces is a diagram that overstates the claim.
end legend

@enduml
